{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TGx8FciiF9VLooyyzfh3PD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good. Now I have all the context I need. Let me write the full LaTeX paper. I'll use a standard article class with appropriate formatting for an EMNLP-style paper.\n\nLet me carefully structure the paper with all the data from experiment_data.json.\n\nKey data points to include:\n\n**Qwen-2.5-7B Results (from framing/C4):**\n- Training GAR: 0.02→0.48\n- Inference Dongre GAR: 0.016\n\n\n- 30x performance gap between training and inference\n- Four-way evaluation shows consistent GAR across TG/UG/LO conditions (0.486-0.492) with stable perplexity around 2.63\n- Phase 1 training achieved 70.8% raw GAR drop with 21% normalized improvement\n\n**Qwen Gradient Conflict Analysis:**\n- Cosine similarity progressively improved from -0.089 to -0.042 across phases\n- Conflict layer percentage decreased from 79% to 67%\n- Magnitude ratio peaked at 7.5x\n\nNow looking at the magnitude ratios escalating dramatically across layers—reaching 14.7x overall with L27 showing extreme 1139x ratio. The attention heatmap reveals goal attention degrading significantly from turn 1 to turn 10, though cross-model correlation remains very high at 0.97.\n\nFor Llama-3.1-8B, Phase 1 GAR started at 0.035 with a 69% relative drop, and training converged to 0.42 with a 26x gap. The three-way evaluation shows TG substantially outperforming both LoRA and base models, while perplexity improvements are dramatic—TG at 2.91 versus base at 4.51.\n\nThe gradient conflict analysis shows cosine similarity improving from -0.076 to -0.038 and magnitude ratios doubling from 8.1x to 16.2x. Bootstrap confidence intervals indicate the differences aren't statistically significant at p=0.71 with negligible effect size. Compliance-GAR correlation is strong at 0.82 with minimal cross-model variance. The exposure bias mitigation strategy achieves a remarkable 7x reduction in the gap, bringing it from 30x down to 4.3x, with eval GAR jumping from 0.016 to 0.072, though perplexity remains slightly higher than the baseline TG approach.\n\nNow I need to synthesize these results into a coherent paper narrative while being rigorous about the formulation—using the ReZero-style x + g·f(x) framework—and ensuring I'm grounding everything in actual empirical findings rather than speculation. I should aim for 25-30 references to properly contextualize this work. I'm continuing to compile the reference list, pulling together papers on model editing, interpretability, and recent large language model architectures that will support the technical arguments.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the page budget for the paper—with an 8-page constraint, I need to allocate space carefully across the abstract, introduction, related work, background, methodology, and experiments sections to ensure each part gets adequate coverage without exceeding the limit.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4117,"cache_read_input_tokens":42201,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4117},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a2fb452f-562d-462f-a389-06a0144388e9","uuid":"609cc146-d8a3-4b5d-a76c-13994fddaa67"}
